# Flight Logbook

> Institutional memory. Concise, factual, high-signal.
> Newest entries first. One block per insight.

## 2026-07-21

### 0526 — Host VPN controller review requests production error-translation rework (TASK-260715-1rsqrh)
- REVIEW: Focused controller/repository tests pass 57/57, focused TSan passes 23/23, full core validation passes 275/275, strict Swift format and diff checks pass, board validation passes, and both iOS/macOS adapter builds succeed when run sequentially.
- REGRESSION: `IOSVPNHostSession.startTunnel` and `MacOSVPNHostSession.startTunnel` forward the public `NETunnelProviderSession.startTunnel(options:)` thrown `NSError` unchanged, while `VPNSessionController.performStart` maps only `VPNPreferencePlatformError` / `VPNPlatformError` and otherwise erases the real domain/code to reflected type plus code 0. The unit test injects a pre-normalized fake error, so the production adapter path is untested and cannot satisfy the accepted exact synchronous `NEVPNError` mapping.
- REWORK: Normalize thrown NetworkExtension start errors at the thin adapter boundary (or through an equivalently shared injectable translator), preserve unknown domain/code, cover both iOS/macOS seams plus the disabled-manager production path, and rerun focused normal/TSan, full core, formatting/diff/board checks, and both adapter builds before another reviewer cycle.

### 0511 — Host VPN control keeps system and provider authorities independent (TASK-260715-1rsqrh)
- DECISION: `OwnedVPNManagerRepository` remains the only ownership/schema authority and now hands out a session only from a freshly loaded exact-owned current manager. `VPNSessionController` retains no manager cache or forwarding state; iOS/macOS wrappers retain the fresh manager only to keep its exact public `NETunnelProviderSession` alive for the command/observer lifetime.
- ORDERING: Start and stop share one locked once-only stop claim, so cancellation, timeout, and a concurrent explicit stop cannot duplicate `stopTunnel`. Controller retirement cancels host waits and observers without claiming that stop; a cancelled stop waiter does not cancel the joined system-stop observation for other callers.
- PROJECTION: Every non-connected system status clears capability immediately. A connected projection accepts only correlated, negotiated lifecycle/capability responses at one matching position strictly newer than the last accepted generation/sequence; relaunch starts without historical app state and asks the provider again. Non-running provider lifecycle states fail safe to zero capabilities.
- ERROR CONTRACT: All 19 public `NEVPNConnectionError` codes, provider codes 1001–1009, unknown domains/codes, provider unknown codes, nil errors, and timeout/cancellation unavailability map to stable finite reasons while system status remains authoritative.
- COVERAGE: Deterministic Swift Testing covers every status, exact v1 start options, synchronous errors, 60/15/3-second deadlines, cancellation/retirement, start-stop races, joined stops, initial observer races, deallocation, nil/late/wrong/corrupt/unsupported/mismatched/stale responses, generation reset, and app recreation.

### 0442 — Apple VPN terminal observation registers before authoritative status (TASK-260715-15vkvz)
- REGRESSION: Both NetworkExtension adapters read `NEVPNConnection.status` before installing `NEVPNStatusDidChange`, so a terminal transition in that gap could be lost and an already-stopped session could falsely reach the 15-second `stopTimedOut` path.
- FIX: iOS and macOS now install the observer first and then perform an authoritative status read through an injectable seam. The locked retirement handshake also handles a synchronous terminal notification before registration returns its token: completion wins once, and the late-arriving token is immediately unregistered.
- COVERAGE: A deterministic two-platform matrix covers terminal-before-registration, terminal-during-registration without a notification, notification-before-token-return, notification-first completion, duplicate/late notification retirement, and cancellation. Every terminal/cancel path unregisters once and produces at most one completion.
- VERIFICATION: Focused normal and Thread Sanitizer runs each pass 33 tests; `make validate-core` passes 251 tests in 24 suites plus build; strict Swift format, tracked/untracked diff, and board validation are clean; generic iOS Simulator and macOS adapter builds succeed with signing disabled.

### 0421 — VPN preference operations are non-reentrant and NSNumber versions are exact (TASK-260715-15vkvz)
- REGRESSION: Actor isolation did not serialize repository calls across suspended NetworkExtension callbacks, so concurrent operations could load and mutate overlapping manager generations. Both platform seams also used `NSNumber.intValue`, truncating `1.5` to current version 1 and overflowing `UInt64.max` into repairable corruption.
- FIX: Every public ensure/enable/disable/remove/repair entry point now crosses one FIFO operation gate, held through its terminal result; internal repair convergence reuses the held operation without re-entering the gate. Manager-contract inspection carries signed `Int` or unsigned `UInt64` versions without narrowing, while Boolean, fractional, and other type-confused versions fail closed with zero mutations.
- COVERAGE: Deterministic held-callback tests queue every repository operation and prove no second load or mutation before the first terminal result; concurrent zero-manager ensure creates and saves exactly one canonical manager. Both iOS and macOS seams cover `NSNumber(true)`, `1.5`, `UInt64.max`, `Int.max`, and `Int(1)`, including zero-write ensure/enable/disable/remove/repair paths.
- VERIFICATION: The focused suite and Thread Sanitizer each pass 27 tests. `make validate-core` passes 245 tests in 24 suites plus the post-test build; strict Swift format lint, diff/board checks, and generic iOS Simulator/macOS adapter builds pass. A first parallel platform-build attempt hit SwiftPM's shared build-database lock; the independent sequential macOS rerun succeeded.

### 0405 — VPN manager rework preserves all future versions and fresh authority (TASK-260715-15vkvz)
- REGRESSION: Future manager-contract versions were narrowed through `UInt16(exactly:)`, so 65,536 and `Int.max` fell into repairable corruption. Explicit enable also returned early when already enabled and could save during connecting, reasserting, or disconnecting.
- FIX: Manager-contract `updateRequired` now carries the original `Int` without narrowing; every version greater than v1 remains unsupported future data. Explicit enable returns `sessionTransitionInProgress` before setters or saves for all three transitions, while an already-enabled manager still performs the user-intent setter/save/fresh-reload verification path.
- FRESHNESS: The preference fake now materializes a distinct manager after every successful save. Stale retries re-evaluate unrelated, unmarked, and `Int.max` future replacements without mutating them, and verification rejects a distinct persisted manager whose canonical fields do not match the requested write.
- VERIFICATION: The focused repository suite passes 23 tests. `make validate-core` passes boundary/native-package checks, 241 tests in 24 suites, and the post-test build; strict Swift format lint, `git diff --check`, and board validation pass. Generic iOS Simulator and macOS adapter builds both succeed.

### 0351 — Owned VPN preferences are shared policy with thin platform clients (TASK-260715-15vkvz)
- DECISION: `ReluxTunnelCore` owns the serialized exact-predicate repository, immutable canonical configuration, 15-second callback tokens, stable errors, one-stale retry, and reload verification. The iOS/macOS adapter modules only translate public `NetworkExtension` objects and notifications into the injectable core seams.
- SAFETY: Ensure never enables; the explicit enable result names the OS-level possibility that another enterprise VPN is disabled. Duplicate repair removes every freshly revalidated current-schema inactive exact-owned manager, reloads after each removal, proves zero, then recreates one. Unmarked, unrelated, wrong-type, corrupt duplicate, active duplicate, and future-owned managers are zero-write paths.
- IDENTITY: The complete host/provider/App Group/Keychain `PlatformVPNIdentity` remains injectable for deterministic fixtures. Both production factories fail closed until `TASK-260715-ypo7yo` supplies accepted signed bindings; no guessed identifier landed.
- VERIFICATION: 18 deterministic Swift Testing cases cover mixed managers, type confusion, least-data/secret exclusion, idempotency, explicit enable/disable/remove, duplicate repair, future preservation, stale replacement, authorization, save/reload failure, timeout/cancellation late callbacks, and stop timeout. `make validate-core` passes 236 tests in 24 suites plus build; strict format lint and iOS Simulator/macOS Xcode builds pass.

### 0320 — Provider routing and cleanup share one bounded generation owner (TASK-260715-1bp6eu)
- DECISION: Both platform roots expose identical callback seams and delegate to one core actor. V1 accepts only the four read-only commands, requires a request UUID at the provider boundary, keeps at most 256 active and 256 recently completed request IDs, and retires pending once-only reply gates on stop so a late source callback cannot reach another generation.
- CLEANUP: Every generation receives a fixed 64-handle cleanup registry. Stop immediately rejects work and fans out cancellation, then joins one reverse runtime stop behind an injected ten-second budget. Expiry force-closes registered controllable handles and records only the raw Apple reason, one fixed counter/gauge, and the finite `cleanup_deadline_exceeded` token. Provider fatal failure emits one stable 1001–1009 NSError and the later system stop joins the same cleanup.
- ORDERING: Stop during factory/runtime startup wins, retires late-created handles, and completes the pending Apple start gate before each stop gate. The 60-second startup race and cleanup race use injected clocks and continuations; no correctness sleep or unbounded task-group join is used.
- SCHEMA: Provider stop metadata extends diagnostics v1 to 97 counters, 57 gauges, and 11 finite error codes across the same 10 domains. The schema still retains no endpoints, DNS names, traffic identifiers, secrets, configuration, localized errors, or free-form labels.
- COVERAGE: Shared tests run through iOS and macOS seams for all four commands, hostile/oversized/future/mutating inputs, duplicates, nil handlers, retirement, all raw Apple reasons, startup and cleanup deadlines, stop-wins-start, fatal handoff, deallocation, and two 100-cycle baselines. Existing coordinator tests retain every startup acquisition/cancellation point and route-clear uncertainty coverage.

### 0245 — VPN lifecycle separates ownership, enablement, system state, and runtime capability (TASK-260715-1q4qhw)
- DECISION: A manager is owned only when its protocol is `NETunnelProviderProtocol`, its provider bundle ID exactly matches the accepted platform binding, and its stable provider-configuration marker matches. Labels, server address, enabled state, profile UUID, schema version, and array order never establish ownership. Every write/remove rechecks a freshly loaded object; unmarked lookalikes and duplicates are zero-write conflicts outside explicit inactive duplicate repair.
- PLATFORM FINDING: Apple limits `loadAllFromPreferences` to configurations created by the calling app, but enabling an enterprise VPN can cause the OS to disable another app's enterprise VPN. Therefore ordinary ensure preserves/creates a disabled configuration and explicit foreground user intent owns enable/save/reload. Relux never mutates the unrelated manager object and reports the system-level enable effect truthfully.
- AUTHORITY: `NETunnelProviderSession.status` is the sole system-session authority; a fresh generation/sequence provider snapshot is the sole runtime capability authority. Host suspension/termination removes observers only. Provider start/stop once-gates and exact deadlines remain extension-owned and join the accepted coordinator cleanup.
- RESIDUAL: Exact four-target bundle IDs, App Group, Keychain group, and entitlements are not yet accepted (`TASK-260715-ypo7yo`). Shared implementation proceeds through injected identities, while production composition fails closed instead of inventing values. Human ratification stays decoupled in `TASK-260717-1dsqnj`.

### 0211 — Coordinator fault suite freezes ownership boundaries and resource baselines (TASK-260715-32virr)
- DECISION: Fault injection uses the coordinator's injected cancellation-check seam to fail at fourteen exact before/after ownership boundaries. This keeps production code unchanged while proving reverse rollback, one disconnecting publication, one terminal publication, exact error mapping, and single cleanup for configuration, SSH, TCP, DNS, packet plane, settings, and packet-read ownership.
- COVERAGE: The explicit control table covers rejected duplicate starts, idempotent stops, healthy/stale/late callbacks in usable, stopping, disconnected, and failed states; phase gates now include configuration as well as SSH, TCP, DNS, packet preparation, settings apply, packet activation, and final health. All four mandatory dependency failures must revoke capability before cleanup begins.
- BASELINE: The fixture now tracks tasks, timers, sockets, channels, and retained dependencies separately. One hundred factory generations restore every counter plus the coordinator's internal task/resource footprint; thirty-two concurrent starts produce one usable publication, and thirty-two repeated stop/health race generations clean each owned resource exactly once.
- REVIEW FINDING: The first pass asserted only the broad coordinator error type for injected startup faults, so incorrect redacted domain/code mappings could pass. Rework freezes the exact error for all eleven startup rows, adds the committed-but-error settings disposition with exactly one clear, and directly covers `startupFailure` and `providerFailure` stop mappings. Start attempts are now explicitly rejected while stopping and after stopped or failed terminal states.
- VERIFICATION: The focused 21-test suite passed 25 consecutive runs and Thread Sanitizer with no race report. Coordinator coverage is 95.48% lines / 92.25% regions. `make validate-core` passed 213 tests in 23 suites plus the post-test build; strict Swift format lint, no-sleep audit, diff check, and board validation passed.

### 0150 — M1 coordinator terminally consumes a stop-before-start generation (TASK-260715-3tlgwm)
- REGRESSION: `stop()` treated the coordinator's initial disconnected state as an idempotent no-op, so it retained the profile reference and left the one-shot generation startable after stop returned.
- FIX: The lifecycle now models one `generationConsumed` invariant shared by start and stop. A winning pre-start stop records termination, enters stopping, and runs the existing single cleanup owner; this releases the configuration reference before returning and makes every later start reject with `generationAlreadyConsumed`.
- COVERAGE: Deterministic direct and concurrent stop-wins tests assert one stopping-to-disconnected publication cycle, repeat-stop idempotency, no configuration load/settings/capability publication, no external resources, and the internal baseline footprint. The concurrent test pauses snapshot publication after the terminal state is recorded, allowing a competing start to enter during the in-flight stop without a production test hook.
- VERIFICATION: The focused suite passes 15 tests normally and under Thread Sanitizer. `make validate-core` passes boundary/native checks, 207 tests in 23 suites, and post-test `swift build`; strict recursive Swift format lint, tracked/untracked whitespace checks, and board validation pass.

### 0140 — M1 coordinator closes the startup-completion ownership race (TASK-260715-3tlgwm)
- REGRESSION: A mandatory-health callback could enter after the tracked startup child returned but before the outer `start()` continuation resumed. Because `startupTask` was still retained, the callback deferred cleanup; the outer continuation then cleared the handle and returned success without revalidating actor state, leaving the generation parked in stopping with retained resources.
- FIX: After joining the startup child, `start()` now rechecks caller cancellation, environment cancellation, lifecycle ownership, and termination while actor-isolated before atomically clearing `startupTask` and returning. A callback that wins the handoff therefore forces the error/cleanup path; a callback that arrives after the atomic handoff observes no startup owner and starts cleanup itself.
- COVERAGE: A package-internal async handoff hook, following the existing core test-hook pattern, deterministically injects current-generation DNS health loss after child completion and independently pauses the same boundary for caller cancellation. Neither test issues a masking `stop()`; both assert no capability revival, exactly-once reverse cleanup, a zero external resource ledger, and the coordinator baseline footprint.
- VERIFICATION: The focused suite passes 13 tests normally and under Thread Sanitizer. `make validate-core` passes boundary/native checks, 205 tests in 23 suites, and post-test `swift build`; strict recursive Swift format lint, `git diff --check`, and board validation pass.

### 0120 — M1 coordinator makes settings uncertainty and cleanup ordering explicit (TASK-260715-3tlgwm)
- IMPLEMENTATION: `TunnelRuntimeCoordinator` owns one actor-serialized generation and a tracked startup task. It retains each successful acquisition before the next cancellation check, rejects repeat starts, discards mismatched-generation health events, and coalesces caller cancellation, provider stop, startup failure, and mandatory health loss through one shielded cleanup task.
- SAFETY: Settings are requested only after validated configuration, authenticated SSH, TCP readiness, SSH-only safe DNS readiness, pure packet-plane preparation, and mandatory health checks. Unknown apply failures are conservatively treated as an uncertain commit and cleared; definite non-commit skips clear. A clear failure publishes TCP/DNS false while truthfully retaining `routesInstalled=true` and `routeState=clearFailed`.
- CLEANUP: Teardown closes admission, stops packet reads/native work, clears settings when required, then stops DNS, TCP, and SSH in reverse acquisition order. The coordinator releases configuration references and all tracked task/resource handles; repeated stop calls join the same cleanup.
- VERIFICATION: Ten injected startup failures, seven stop/cancellation boundaries, caller cancellation, stale callbacks, 32 concurrent stops, clear failure, snapshot ordering, and 100 generations pass in normal and Thread Sanitizer runs. `make validate-core` passes boundary/native checks, 203 tests in 23 suites, and the post-test build; strict repository Swift format lint, `git diff --check`, and board validation pass.

